On Jun 17, 10:57=A0am, Karl Garrison <kdgar...@gmail.com> wrote:
> Can anyone thing of an instance of a menu in NetHack that has one or
> more items preselected? =A0The only instance I can find in the code is
> in spell.c for the spell-casting menu, but I can't figure out in what
> instance a spell on there would be preselected. =A0Does anyone know this
> offhand, or know of another instance?

     When you use the '+' command to examine your known spells,
you can type a spell's letter and the program will prompt you
for another known spell to swap with the one you've picked,
providing a limited way to control the spell letters.  It uses
the preselected attribute when you've given that first letter,
at the time it's prompting for the second one.

     That appears to be the only instance in the core code;
I didn't look through the various sets of interface code.
The development version has a few more instances in the core.
Menu implementation doesn't support preselection very well,
at least in the way I've tried to use it.  If the user
explicitly picks a pre-selected entry, it gets toggled back
to unselected.  That could be useful for PICK_ANY menus
(except that there aren't any instances of using pre-selection
for those) but is not very useful for PICK_ONE menus (where
all the actual uses occur).  For spell sorting, this isn't a
big deal; unselecting the previously picked spell just cancels
the swap currently in progress.  But for the newer usage where
one entry must be picked, the code ends up checking for a
selection of zero items and then uses the original preselected
one even though it got unselected.  It also has to check for
a selection of two items, since picking some other entry
*doesn't* toggle off the preselected one.  (Messy and no doubt
bug-prone, but changing how menus actually work requires
modifying all the various interface-specific implementations,
which is pretty difficult to test and debug.)


